; Berzerk.
; See docs/berzerk-design.md.
;
;   load "berzerk
;   berzerk
;
; M1 IS THE ROOM, and the room is all
; that is here yet. Arrows walk from one
; room to the next, ESC quits. There is
; no man, no robot and no bolt: those
; are M2, M3 and M4, and each one is a
; section of the design document.
;
; THE MILESTONE'S GATE is one sentence,
; and you close it with your fingers:
; the maze is a function of ROOM.X and
; ROOM.Y, so walk out of a room and back
; into it and it is the same room.
; Nothing is kept between the two. The
; maze is thrown away at the doorway and
; generated again from the room's own
; coordinates, which is why an infinite
; maze costs two globals and no storage
; at all (design section 6.1). It is the
; best thing in the Berzerk ROM and it
; ports for nothing.
;
; SO THE FIFTEEN WALL MASKS ARE ON THE
; SCREEN, under the picture, laid out
; five across and three down the way
; design section 6.3 prints them. Two
; rooms can look alike at a glance and
; their masks cannot, so walking out and
; back is a PROOF when the fifteen
; numbers come back identical and an
; impression when only the drawing does.
;
; The masks earn their place twice over.
; They are what the robots will consult
; in M3 -- a wall test is "which cell am
; I in, is the edge I am crossing
; walled", four statements and no pixels
; -- so if the table and the picture
; ever disagree, a robot walks through a
; wall you can see. Putting both on the
; screen together is the cheapest guard
; this milestone can carry, and it costs
; three text rows the game does not need
; until M6.
;
; DESIGN QUESTION 3, ANSWERED HERE
; because M1 is where the room
; coordinates live: the maze WRAPS at
; 256 in each axis. ROOM_X and ROOM_Y
; are one byte each in the cabinet
; ($4345 holds ROOM_X in L and ROOM_Y in
; H), and the seed is ROOM_X plus 256
; times ROOM_Y -- so a coordinate
; outside 0..255 is not a room the
; cabinet can be in.
; Letting them run instead would alias
; badly rather than usefully: room
; (256, 0) and room (0, 1) share a seed
; and are therefore the same room, which
; is a wrap nobody chose. `modulo` takes
; the sign of its divisor, so one call
; folds -1 to 255 and 256 to 0, and the
; whole decision is two lines.
;
; THE KEYBOARD IS READ AS A CHARACTER
; STREAM, not as key state. Design
; section 8.2 puts the game on
; `pollkeys` and `keydown?` and it is
; right about the GAME: a man who walks
; while you hold an arrow wants key
; state, and M2 brings it. This
; milestone is turn based -- one press
; is one room -- and key state read in a
; loop with nothing else to do reports
; the same press three or four times, so
; one tap would walk you three rooms
; away. The Snake Temple lost a
; milestone to exactly that.
;
; AND THE TRANSITION IS TIMED, on the
; two text rows below the masks, because
; a room change is the one hitch this
; game has that is not a frame. Read on
; a Pico 2 W on 2026-08-30: 30 ms at
; `fast`, worst 33, and 44 at `normal`,
; worst 53 -- M0's parts (7.37 ms to
; generate, 3.34 to draw, plus a
; full-canvas present) to within 3 %.
;
; The two clocks are what makes the
; reading worth keeping: 1.47x rather
; than 2x, so 13.2 ms of a room change
; is CPU and 16.8 is the SPI wire. More
; than half of it is the panel, and
; nothing this file could be written
; differently would touch that half.
;
; A number on this display cannot be
; copied anywhere else, so any new
; reading goes into the roadmap by
; hand, with the clock beside it.

;=====================================
; The playfield, at 1:1 with the cabinet
; (design section 5)
;
;   turtle x = arcade x - 126
;   turtle y = 142 - arcade y
;
; 244 arcade pixels of 320 across and
; 204 of the 240 rows `splitscreen`
; presents, centred at (0, +40). Every
; constant in the disassembly then
; transfers verbatim in x and NEGATED in
; y, and that sign is the one thing here
; worth checking twice.
;
; The 5 x 3 cell grid: column boundaries
; at turtle x = -70, -22, 26, 74 and row
; boundaries at turtle y = 74, 6.

; White, as the cabinet's walls are at
; difficulty 0.
make "wall.pc 254

; WINDOW, not the default `wrap`. It
; costs nothing to set now and M2 cannot
; do without it: the man's contact box
; hangs four steps past him, and at the
; right-hand wall a wrapped probe lands
; on the far side of the screen.
window

;=====================================
; The room (design section 6)

make "room.x 0
make "room.y 0
make "seed   0

; MAZE_ZONES, 15 bytes, one per cell,
; five wide and row-major:
;
;   bit 0 (1) = wall on this cell's LEFT
;   bit 1 (2) = wall on its RIGHT
;   bit 2 (4) = wall on its TOP
;   bit 3 (8) = wall on its BOTTOM
;
; Built with `list` and never written as
; a literal in a procedure body: a
; literal belongs to the stored body of
; whatever holds it, and `.setitem`
; would scribble on it for good.
make "cell []

; The eight interior segments, one per
; interior grid intersection: start
; point in turtle steps, heading and
; length. Four lists of eight rather
; than thirty-two names, which is design
; section 18's arithmetic on the
; 254-global ceiling.
make "seg.x (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)
make "seg.y (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)
make "seg.h (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)
make "seg.l (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)

; The generator at $2678: a 16-bit LCG
; whose output is the high byte. It has
; to be THIS one and not `random`,
; because the whole point is reproducing
; the room, and `rerandom` reseeds a
; different stream that is not
; indexable by room coordinate.
;
; 7 * 65535 + 12627 is 471,372, well
; inside the 2^24 a single-precision
; float holds exactly, so the modulo is
; not rounding anything away.
to rnd
  make "seed modulo ((7 * :seed) + 12627) 65536
  output int (:seed / 256)
end

to wall.set :idx :bit
  .setitem :idx :cell bitor (item :idx :cell) :bit
end

; The template at $268C, `ldir`-copied
; into MAZE_ZONES at $2567 before the
; eight segments set their bits into it.
;
; THE BORDER CELLS ARE WALLED ON ALL
; FOUR OUTER SIDES EVEN WHERE THE EXITS
; ARE -- cell (row 1, col 0) carries a
; LEFT wall although the left doorway is
; exactly there. That is not a bug. The
; table is what ROBOTS consult, and
; robots never leave the room; the
; player's exits are his own position
; test (design section 8.3, M2), which
; is why the arcade needs both.
to wall.template
  output (list 5 4 4 4 6 1 0 0 0 2 9 8 8 8 10)
end

; One intersection. `c` is the column
; boundary 1-4 (between cells c-1 and c)
; and `r` the row boundary 1-2; `k` is
; which of the eight slots this segment
; goes in.
;
; The four choices are the `set`
; instructions at $2617-$264B against
; ix+0, ix+1, ix+5 and ix+6 -- an index
; into the 5-wide array where +1 is the
; next column and +5 the next row, which
; is what makes the mapping legible:
;
;   rand & 3 = 2  horizontal, one cell
;     RIGHT: bottom of above-right, top
;     of below-right
;   rand & 3 = 3  horizontal, one cell
;     LEFT: bottom of above-left, top of
;     below-left
;   rand & 3 = 0  vertical, one cell
;     ABOVE: right of above-left, left
;     of above-right
;   rand & 3 = 1  vertical, one cell
;     BELOW: right of below-left, left
;     of below-right
;
; That is the entire maze generator.
; Nothing checks connectivity and
; nothing prevents a sealed pocket, and
; the rooms it makes are the rooms
; everybody remembers.
to room.seg :k :c :r
  local "w local "ix local "iy
  local "a local "b
  make "w bitand rnd 3
  make "ix (48 * :c) - 118
  make "iy 142 - (68 * :r)
  make "a (5 * (:r - 1)) + :c
  make "b (5 * :r) + :c
  .setitem :k :seg.x :ix
  .setitem :k :seg.y :iy
  if 2 = :w [
    .setitem :k :seg.h 90
    .setitem :k :seg.l 48
    wall.set (:a + 1) 8
    wall.set (:b + 1) 4
    stop]
  if 3 = :w [
    .setitem :k :seg.h 270
    .setitem :k :seg.l 48
    wall.set :a 8
    wall.set :b 4
    stop]
  if 0 = :w [
    .setitem :k :seg.h 0
    .setitem :k :seg.l 68
    wall.set :a 2
    wall.set (:a + 1) 1
    stop]
  .setitem :k :seg.h 180
  .setitem :k :seg.l 68
  wall.set :b 2
  wall.set (:b + 1) 1
end

; RNG_SEED := ROOM_X + 256.ROOM_Y at the
; top of every room build ($2540), so
; the maze -- and, from M3, the robot
; placement that follows it -- is a pure
; function of where you are.
;
; CREATE_ROOM ($25EB) is called twice,
; once per interior row boundary, and
; each call steps x by 48 while x < 220,
; so x = 56, 104, 152, 200. Eight
; intersections in all, and THE ORDER
; MATTERS: it is the order the generator
; is drawn from, and the room only
; reproduces if it is kept.
to setup.room
  make "seed :room.x + (256 * :room.y)
  make "cell wall.template
  room.seg 1 1 1
  room.seg 2 2 1
  room.seg 3 3 1
  room.seg 4 4 1
  room.seg 5 1 2
  room.seg 6 2 2
  room.seg 7 3 2
  room.seg 8 4 2
end

; WALLINDEX ($1CE7): a point to a cell
; index, 1-based. This is the room's
; whole public interface from M2 on --
; the cabinet did wall collision by
; DRAWING, with sprites XOR-ed into
; magic RAM and a hardware intercept
; bit, and we have no such bit and must
; not fake one with `colourunder`.
;
; The clamps are not defensive padding.
; M3's `iq` probes points four steps
; outside a robot, which at a wall are
; outside the playfield, and the arcade
; clamps there too.
to cell.at :x :y
  local "c local "r
  make "c int ((:x + 118) / 48)
  make "r int ((142 - :y) / 68)
  if 0 > :c [make "c 0]
  if :c > 4 [make "c 4]
  if 0 > :r [make "r 0]
  if :r > 2 [make "r 2]
  output (5 * :r) + :c + 1
end

;=====================================
; The walls (design section 7.1)
;
; Sixteen runs at most: eight of border
; and up to eight interior. The border
; is one closed circuit with the pen
; toggling -- twelve statements and no
; `setpos` after the first, against
; sixteen `setpos`-and-`fd` pairs if
; each run were placed on its own.
;
; The doorway is the middle cell of each
; side: 48 steps wide top and bottom, 68
; tall left and right. The top run is
; 100 + 48 + 96 rather than a symmetric
; 98 + 48 + 98 because the cabinet's
; leftmost column is 52 arcade pixels
; and the other four are 48.
;
; PEN SIZE STAYS 1. A 2-pixel pen reads
; better and costs round caps that spill
; outside the stroke, which is the
; artefact that once made a present-cost
; harness measure every frame as a full
; screen (docs/hardware-notes.md 9.1).
to draw.border
  pu setpos [-122 142] seth 90 pd
  fd 100  pu fd 48  pd fd 96
  rt 90
  fd 68  pu fd 68  pd fd 68
  rt 90
  fd 96  pu fd 48  pd fd 100
  rt 90
  fd 68  pu fd 68  pd fd 68
  pu
end

to draw.walls
  local "i
  draw.border
  repeat 8 [
    make "i repcount
    pu setx item :i :seg.x
    sety item :i :seg.y
    seth item :i :seg.h
    pd fd item :i :seg.l
  ]
  pu
end

;=====================================
; Walking out, and walking back
;
; The whole milestone is the four
; procedures below, and the timing is on
; the one that does the work: generate
; the room, clear the canvas, draw the
; walls, present. `clean` and not `cs`,
; because `cs` restores automatic
; refresh and would quietly end the
; measurement.

make "room.ms  0    ; the last one
make "worst.ms 0    ; and the worst

to draw.room
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  setup.room
  clean
  setpc :wall.pc
  draw.walls
  refresh
  make "room.ms ticks - :t0
  if :room.ms > :worst.ms [
    make "worst.ms :room.ms]
end

; Design question 3, taken here: the
; coordinates wrap at 256, because the
; cabinet holds each in a byte and a
; seed of ROOM_X + 256.ROOM_Y has no
; room outside 0..255 to name.
to go.room :dx :dy
  make "room.x modulo (:room.x + :dx) 256
  make "room.y modulo (:room.y + :dy) 256
  draw.room
  show.room
end

; The fifteen masks in their 5 x 3
; layout, each in a three-column field.
; A mask is 0 to 15, so a number is one
; digit or two and the trailing space is
; the erase: "10 " covers three columns
; and "9 " covers the first two of them,
; leaving the third blank from last
; time.
to show.masks
  local "r local "c
  repeat 3 [
    make "r repcount
    repeat 5 [
      make "c repcount
      setcursor list 3 * (:c - 1) 25 + :r
      (type item (5 * (:r - 1)) + :c :cell "| |)
    ]
  ]
end

to show.room
  setcursor [0 25]
  (type "|ROOM | :room.x "| | :room.y "|    |)
  show.masks
  setcursor [20 26]
  (type "|ROOM MS | :room.ms "|  |)
  setcursor [20 27]
  (type "|WORST   | :worst.ms "|  |)
  setcursor [0 29]
  type "|ARROWS WALK OUT, ESC QUITS|
end

;=====================================
; The session

; The codes `readchar` hands back. The
; arrows have no printable character, so
; they arrive as codes of their own; the
; `keydown?` entry in the reference
; lists them all.
make "key.left  180
make "key.right 183
make "key.up    181
make "key.down  182
make "key.esc   177

make "leaving false

; One press is one room. Up is ROOM_Y
; MINUS one because arcade y runs down
; the screen -- the exit table at $2157
; sends `y < 2` to ROOM_Y - 1 -- and
; that is the same negated y as
; everything else here.
to walk
  local "k
  until [:leaving] [
    make "k ascii readchar
    if :k = :key.left  [go.room -1 0]
    if :k = :key.right [go.room 1 0]
    if :k = :key.up    [go.room 0 -1]
    if :k = :key.down  [go.room 0 1]
    if :k = :key.esc   [make "leaving true]
  ]
end

; ONE EXIT, and it puts the screen back
; the way it found it -- `setrefresh
; "auto` above all, because a session
; left in manual mode looks like a
; broken interpreter to whoever types
; the next command.
;
; There is no key drain here and there
; is nothing to drain: the game is
; entered by typing its name and Enter,
; which the REPL has already taken, and
; M6's attract screen is where a
; keypress can outlive the screen that
; asked for it.
to berzerk
  splitscreen
  window
  setrefresh "manual
  ct
  make "worst.ms 0
  make "leaving false
  draw.room
  show.room
  walk
  setrefresh "auto
  textscreen
  ct
end
